0.40.850.051 D Parsed message: {"role":"assistant","content":"{\"action\":\"replace_file\",\"content\":\"# Adaptive Alerts and Overnight Retention: A Multisite Evaluation\\n\\n## Abstract\\n\\nIn a prospective matched-cohort evaluation across six wards, adaptive alerts were associated with an 18.4 percentage-point higher rate of overnight retention. Retention was 71.2% with adaptive alerts and 52.8% under usual monitoring (adjusted difference 18.4 percentage points; 95% CI, 6.1 to 30.7) [D1][D2]. The nonrandomized design does not establish causation, and differential sensor dropout could bias the estimated association upward [D1][D3].\\n\\n## Introduction\\n\\nLoss of overnight sensor observations can delay recognition of clinical deterioration. Adaptive alerts may improve record retention by identifying interrupted streams before the observation window closes [D1].\\n\\nWe evaluated whether adaptive alerts were associated with retention of usable overnight sensor records across six inpatient wards.\\n\\n## Methods\\n\\nWe conducted a prospective matched-cohort evaluation across six wards. Wards introduced adaptive alerts according to local operational schedules; patients were not randomly assigned. Each intervention observation was matched to a usual-monitoring observation by ward and baseline risk.\\n\\nThe primary endpoint was retention of a usable overnight sensor record through 06:00. The analysis used complete cases and did not impute missing sensor records. Ward-level subgroup comparisons were exploratory.\\n\\n## Results\\n\\nOvernight retention was 71.2% with adaptive alerts and 52.8% with usual monitoring, an adjusted absolute difference of 18.4 percentage points (95% CI, 6.1 to 30.7; p = 0.006) [D2].\\n\\nSensor dropout affected 8.7% of adaptive-alert observations and 3.1% of usual-monitoring observations. The primary analysis used complete cases without imputation, and the differential dropout could bias the estimated association upward [D3].\\n\\nWard-level subgroup comparisons were exploratory and were not preregistered; they do not establish effect heterogeneity [D3].\\n\\n## Discussion\\n\\nThis matched-cohort evaluation found that adaptive alerts were associated with higher overnight retention, but the nonrandomized design does not establish causation [D1][D2]. Differential sensor dropout could bias the estimated association upward, so the observed difference should be interpreted cautiously [D3]. The ward-level subgroup analysis was exploratory and not preregistered, and it does not provide confirmatory evidence of effect heterogeneity [D3].\\n\\n## References\\n\\n[D1] Operations Consortium. Multisite rollout design record. 2026.\\n\\n[D2] Analysis Group. Revised overnight-retention model. 2026.\\n\\n[D3] Data Quality Team. Sensor-completeness audit. 2026.\\n\",\"expected_file_sha256\":\"8e72b40dcdd3597319eb62f1ae030666cebffc7d5798acd82f80b79d381a6439\",\"path\":\"paper.md\"}"}
