ments to `[D3]`; and\n- add no source, statistic, or recommendation not supported by the locked\n  records.\n\nPreserve the title and the complete Introduction, Methods, and References\nsections of `paper.md` verbatim. Keep the existing section headings in their\nexisting order. Do not change `evidence_update.md` or `editorial_memo.md`.\nOnly `paper.md` should change.\n\nThe declared `visible` check covers candidate file identity, locked-source and\nprotected-section preservation, section structure, obsolete literal markers,\nand citation syntax. External grading evaluates the revised evidence claims\nand cross-section consistency. Submit when you choose to finish.\n\n\nINITIAL EXACT WORK SURFACE\n{\"candidate_id\":\"37d9c7dbf295039a3763cd675ebedc59a6c95874b33b870e9657e104c5583adc\",\"cell_id\":\"D2\",\"exact_external_history\":true,\"exact_results_reopenable_after_demotion\":true,\"mutable_paths\":[\"paper.md\"],\"schema_version\":\"capability-terrain-initial-surface-v0\",\"visible_check_id\":\"visible\"}<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n{\n    \"action\": \"tree\",\n    \"path\": \".\"\n}<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nEXACT ACTION RESULT\n{\"accepted\":true,\"candidate_id\":\"37d9c7dbf295039a3763cd675ebedc59a6c95874b33b870e9657e104c5583adc\",\"entries\":[{\"bytes\":867,\"path\":\"editorial_memo.md\",\"sha256\":\"11eb5a7991372eaff8f72e3dc6a06606413350a992f639923d46b8031665885e\",\"type\":\"file\"},{\"bytes\":1031,\"path\":\"evidence_update.md\",\"sha256\":\"b329c4288fb9da0d51d52ba0480ea23494673d85147bdea7ad03f947d7b0f6d5\",\"type\":\"file\"},{\"bytes\":2258,\"path\":\"paper.md\",\"sha256\":\"8e72b40dcdd3597319eb62f1ae030666cebffc7d5798acd82f80b79d381a6439\",\"type\":\"file\"}],\"path\":\".\",\"result_id\":\"RESULT-001\",\"schema_version\":\"exact-action-result-v0\",\"tool\":\"tree\"}<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n{\"action\":\"read\",\"path\":\"paper.md\"}<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nEXACT ACTION RESULT\n{\"accepted\":true,\"bytes\":2258,\"candidate_id\":\"37d9c7dbf295039a3763cd675ebedc59a6c95874b33b870e9657e104c5583adc\",\"content\":\"# Adaptive Alerts and Overnight Retention: A Multisite Evaluation\\n\\n## Abstract\\n\\nIn a randomized trial across six wards, adaptive alerts caused a 12.0 percentage-point increase in overnight retention. Retention was 64.8% with adaptive alerts and 52.8% under usual monitoring. These findings demonstrate that adaptive alerts improve retention and support immediate deployment across inpatient settings [D2].\\n\\n## Introduction\\n\\nLoss of overnight sensor observations can delay recognition of clinical deterioration. Adaptive alerts may improve record retention by identifying interrupted streams before the observation window closes [D1].\\n\\nWe evaluated whether adaptive alerts were associated with retention of usable overnight sensor records across six inpatient wards.\\n\\n## Methods\\n\\nWe conducted a prospective matched-cohort evaluation across six wards. Wards introduced adaptive alerts according to local operational schedules; patients were not randomly assigned. Each intervention observation was matched to a usual-monitoring observation by ward and baseline risk.\\n\\nThe primary endpoint was retention of a usable overnight sensor record through 06:00. The analysis used complete cases and did not impute missing sensor records. Ward-level subgroup comparisons were exploratory.\\n\\n## Results\\n\\nOvernight retention was 64.8% with adaptive alerts and 52.8% with usual monitoring, an adjusted absolute difference of 12.0 percentage points (95% CI, 3.2 to 20.8; p = 0.01) [D2].\\n\\nThe improvement was largest on medical wards. This preregistered subgroup confirms that ward type modifies the alert effect [D3].\\n\\nSensor dropout was negligible (<2% in both groups) and cannot explain the observed benefit [D3].\\n\\n## Discussion\\n\\nThis evaluation establishes that adaptive alerts caused higher overnight retention and supports immediate deployment across inpatient settings [D1][D2].\\n\\nThe preregistered ward subgroup provides confirmatory evidence that the intervention is most effective on medical wards [D3]. Sensor missingness was too rare to affect interpretation.\\n\\n## References\\n\\n[D1] Operations Consortium. Multisite rollout design record. 2026.\\n\\n[D2] Analysis Group. Revised overnight-retention model. 2026.\\n\\n[D3] Data Quality Team. Sensor-completeness audit. 2026.\\n\",\"file_sha256\":\"8e72b40dcdd3597319eb62f1ae030666cebffc7d5798acd82f80b79d381a6439\",\"path\":\"paper.md\",\"result_id\":\"RESULT-002\",\"schema_version\":\"exact-action-result-v0\",\"tool\":\"read\"}<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n{\"action\":\"read\",\"path\":\"evidence_update.md\"}<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nEXACT ACTION RESULT\n{\"accepted\":true,\"bytes\":1031,\"candidate_id\":\"37d9c7dbf295039a3763cd675ebedc59a6c95874b33b870e9657e104c5583adc\",\"content\":\"# Locked evidence update\\n\\nThis file is a source record. Do not edit it.\\n\\n## [D1] Design record\\n\\n- The evaluation covered six inpatient wards.\\n- It was a prospective matched-cohort evaluation based on local rollout\\n  schedules, not a randomized trial.\\n- The design supports association language but does not by itself establish\\n  causation.\\n\\n## [D2] Revised primary analysis\\n\\n- Overnight retention was 71.2% with adaptive alerts and 52.8% with usual\\n  monitoring.\\n- The adjusted absolute difference was 18.4 percentage points.\\n- The 95% confidence interval was 6.1 to 30.7 percentage points.\\n- The two-sided p value was 0.006.\\n\\n## [D3] Completeness and subgroup audit\\n\\n- Sensor dropout affected 8.7% of adaptive-alert observations and 3.1% of\\n  usual-monitoring observations.\\n- The primary analysis used complete cases without imputation. Differential\\n  dropout could bias the estimated association upward.\\n- Ward-level subgroup comparisons were exploratory and were not preregistered.\\n  They do not establish effect heterogeneity.\\n\",\"file_sha256\":\"b329c4288fb9da0d51d52ba0480ea23494673d85147bdea7ad03f947d7b0f6d5\",\"path\":\"evidence_update.md\",\"result_id\":\"RESULT-003\",\"schema_version\":\"exact-action-result-v0\",\"tool\":\"read\"}<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n{\"action\":\"read\",\"path\":\"editorial_memo.md\"}<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nEXACT ACTION RESULT\n{\"accepted\":true,\"bytes\":867,\"candidate_id\":\"37d9c7dbf295039a3763cd675ebedc59a6c95874b33b870e9657e104c5583adc\",\"content\":\"# Locked editorial memo\\n\\nThis file is an editorial source record. Do not edit it.\\n\\nRevise only `paper.md`.\\n\\n- In the Abstract, identify the matched-cohort design, replace the superseded\\n  primary result, and name both the design and dropout limitations.\\n- In Results, report the revised primary result and the completeness audit.\\n  Remove the unsupported directional subgroup claim and state the subgroup's\\n  exploratory status.\\n- In Discussion, align the claim strength with the nonrandomized design,\\n  explain the possible upward bias from differential dropout, and treat the\\n  subgroup analysis as nonconfirmatory.\\n- Cite `[D1]` for design, `[D2]` for the primary result, and `[D3]` for\\n  missingness and subgroup status. Do not add sources.\\n- Do not recommend deployment from this evaluation.\\n- Preserve the title, Introduction, Methods, and References verbatim.\\n\",\"file_sha256\":\"11eb5a7991372eaff8f72e3dc6a06606413350a992f639923d46b8031665885e\",\"path\":\"editorial_memo.md\",\"result_id\":\"RESULT-004\",\"schema_version\":\"exact-action-result-v0\",\"tool\":\"read\"}<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n{\"action\":\"replace_file\",\"content\":\"# Adaptive Alerts and Overnight Retention: A Multisite Evaluation\\n\\n## Abstract\\n\\nIn a prospective matched-cohort evaluation across six wards, adaptive alerts were associated with an 18.4 percentage-point higher rate of overnight retention. Retention was 71.2% with adaptive alerts and 52.8% under usual monitoring (adjusted difference 18.4 percentage points; 95% CI, 6.1 to 30.7) [D1][D2]. The nonrandomized design does not establish causation, and differential sensor dropout could bias the estimated association upward [D1][D3].\\n\\n## Introduction\\n\\nLoss of overnight sensor observations can delay recognition of clinical deterioration. Adaptive alerts may improve record retention by identifying interrupted streams before the observation window closes [D1].\\n\\nWe evaluated whether adaptive alerts were associated with retention of usable overnight sensor records across six inpatient wards.\\n\\n## Methods\\n\\nWe conducted a prospective matched-cohort evaluation across six wards. Wards introduced adaptive alerts according to local operational schedules; patients were not randomly assigned. Each intervention observation was matched to a usual-monitoring observation by ward and baseline risk.\\n\\nThe primary endpoint was retention of a usable overnight sensor record through 06:00. The analysis used complete cases and did not impute missing sensor records. Ward-level subgroup comparisons were exploratory.\\n\\n## Results\\n\\nOvernight retention was 71.2% with adaptive alerts and 52.8% with usual monitoring, an adjusted absolute difference of 18.4 percentage points (95% CI, 6.1 to 30.7; p = 0.006) [D2].\\n\\nSensor dropout affected 8.7% of adaptive-alert observations and 3.1% of usual-monitoring observations. The primary analysis used complete cases without imputation, and the differential dropout could bias the estimated association upward [D3].\\n\\nWard-level subgroup comparisons were exploratory and were not preregistered; they do not establish effect heterogeneity [D3].\\n\\n## Discussion\\n\\nThis matched-cohort evaluation found that adaptive alerts were associated with higher overnight retention, but the nonrandomized design does not establish causation [D1][D2]. Differential sensor dropout could bias the estimated association upward, so the observed difference should be interpreted cautiously [D3]. The ward-level subgroup analysis was exploratory and not preregistered, and it does not provide confirmatory evidence of effect heterogeneity [D3].\\n\\n## References\\n\\n[D1] Operations Consortium. Multisite rollout design record. 2026.\\n\\n[D2] Analysis Group. Revised overnight-retention model. 2026.\\n\\n[D3] Data Quality Team. Sensor-completeness audit. 2026.\\n\",\"expected_file_sha256\":\"8e72b40dcdd3597319eb62f1ae030666cebffc7d5798acd82f80b79d381a6439\",\"path\":\"paper.md\"}<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nEXACT ACTION RESULT\n{\"accepted\":true,\"bytes_after\":2649,\"candidate_after\":\"6e637d7454117bedbe1f8eb829da969cdd0edef12b9a54f79fb2e80c9b7fb71c\",\"candidate_before\":\"37d9c7dbf295039a3763cd675ebedc59a6c95874b33b870e9657e104c5583adc\",\"file_sha256_after\":\"827bf3188e15d1fe27803367a67cf8fcc8a0f5658a8d77a8e8b49e1237ef64e1\",\"file_sha256_before\":\"8e72b40dcdd3597319eb62f1ae030666cebffc7d5798acd82f80b79d381a6439\",\"path\":\"paper.md\",\"result_id\":\"RESULT-005\",\"schema_version\":\"exact-action-result-v0\",\"tool\":\"replace_file\"}<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\n","generated":""}
